// CARD a0-01 · SEE: the real 1958 brick patent drawing on a dark desk; the camera rides down from the filing header to FIG. 1, the drawn brick; on "today" a plastic brick drops onto the drawing and clicks into place; FILED 1958 and TODAY chips set · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@patent_snap;doc:lego_doc_patent_us3005282_drawing_sheet1;go:today;fx:.32;fy:.27;h:.055;then:PATENT DRAWING · 1958;now:TODAY;out:right"
LEGO says a brick made in nineteen fifty-eight still snaps onto a brick made **today**.

// CARD a0-02 · SEE: two hands pressing small studded plastic bricks together on a light table, the camera pushing in to the click · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@reel;clip:lego_clip_white_bricks_hands;move:in;z:.2;in:right"
That's a promise about precision, kept for more than sixty **years**. Remember that promise.

// CARD a0-03 · SEE: a real pile of LEGO bricks seen from above, dim; on "parts" plastic bricks start raining into the frame and pile up from the floor, more and more, faster than you could count · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@brick_rain;bg:lego_bricks_assorted;go:story;n:170;rate:30;out:right"
This is the story of how a company famous for precision lost count of its own **parts**, and how it got the count back.

// CARD a0-04 · SEE: the camera dollies along a real LEGO shop wall of boxes; on "cost" paper price tags swing in on strings, one per box, each with only a question mark · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@price_query;bg:lego_legoland_shop;go:tell;tags:.18,.42~.36,.5~.55,.4~.72,.52;lab:2003 · PRICE PER SET?;in:right"
{wry} In two thousand and three, LEGO couldn't tell you what its own boxes **cost**. (J)

// CARD a0-05 · SEE: a closed box lands on a dark desk with its small price tag; on "source" its parts pour out beside it into a heap and a much bigger tag drops onto the heap · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@box_vs_parts;bg:lego_technic_supra_chassis;go:source;heap:70;btag:THE BOX'S PRICE;htag:WHAT THE PARTS COST;note:LEGO designer Mark Stafford, 2014 interview · motor and fibre-optic sets;out:right"
One of its designers later said some sets cost more to source than the whole box sold **for**.

// CARD a0-06 · SEE: a night boardroom (AI moment art, no faces): the camera glides down the long table past blurred backs and blank papers · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@reel;under:lego_scene_boardroom_2003;ua:0;move:right;z:.35;slow:knew;in:right"
And no one at LEGO actually **knew**. (J)

// CARD a0-07 · SEE: the real 2005 news page (PBN, carrying Bloomberg) flies in turning over and lands; a highlighter runs across "the net loss widened to 1.93 billion kroner" on "lost" · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@doc_flip;doc:lego_doc_headline_bloomberg_pbn_2005;bg:lego_clip_chart_falling_screens;land:next;mark:lost;line:.358;out:right"
The next year, LEGO lost almost two billion **kroner**.

// CARD a0-08 · SEE: LEGO's old System House office in Billund; on "sell" a red placard on a post is planted in front of it: SELL · the bankers' advice, Nov 2004 · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@sign_on_footage;clip:lego_system_house;sign:SELL;sub:ADVICE · 2004;go:sell;x:.68;y:.42;in:right"
Bankers advised the family to sell the **company**.

// CARD a0-09 · SEE: a real LEGO city diorama dim behind; two towers of plastic bricks build themselves: Mattel and Hasbro stacked in two colours on the left, LEGO on the right climbing taller; $9.52B and $10.85B land on top · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@tower_compare;bg:lego_clip_build_legotown;l:MATTEL^5.38^blue~HASBRO^4.14^white;r:LEGO^10.85^red;step:.7;go:Twenty;rgo:bigger;lab:REVENUE 2024 · US$ BILLIONS (REPORTED);out:right"
{tense} Twenty years later, LEGO was bigger than Mattel and Hasbro **combined**.

// CARD a0-10 · SEE: hard cuts on the words: an empty warehouse on "die", a CLOSING DOWN SALE window on "bankrupt", a pile of bricks on "brick" · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@word_cuts;items:lego_clip_empty_warehouse@die@.5@.5~lego_clip_closing_sale@bankrupt@.5@.45@1.5~lego_brick_color_pile@brick@.5@.45@1.6;in:right"
So how did LEGO nearly die? Everyone says it nearly went bankrupt, and was saved by going back to the **brick**.

// CARD a0-11 · SEE: a man reading a newspaper in the street, dim; WENT BANKRUPT types in and is struck through on "never" (NO FILING · NO COURT); SAVED BY A HIT TOY types and is struck on "saved" · SEAM out right · HOLD
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@myth_strike;clip:lego_clip_newspaper_man;lines:WENT BANKRUPT^never^NO FILING · NO COURT~SAVED BY A HIT TOY^saved;out:right"
The real story is stranger. It never went bankrupt, and no hit toy saved **it**. [BEAT]

// CARD a0-12 · MOTIF · SEE: a bin of red bricks at LEGO House; one brick drops in from above, lands on the ledge with a click and a puff; COUNTING sets above it · SEAM in right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_bricks_legohouse_display;go:counting;x:.66;y:.48;h:.13;lab:COUNTING;lx:.5;z:.25;in:right"
It was saved by **counting**.
